## external_validation_rover | show: ['data_and_limits'] | status: None
    What was checked against known places (Rover only): the mobility analog sites named in rover_preset.py, with coordinates checked in our benchmark, are placed in the full ranking of 10,337 points as follows: Bardenas (top 68.2 %), Tabernas (top 71.5 %), MDRS (top 20.5 %), Yungay (top 8.1 %), Dhofar (does not pass the funnel: slope_300m_deg outside [0.5, 15.0], rough_300m_m outside [5.0, 650.0]), Ramon (top 3.6 %), Haughton (top 4.2 %) and Qaidam (top 4.0 %). Sites named there as mismatches: Concordia (does not pass the funnel: slope_300m_deg outside [0.5, 15.0], rough_300m_m outside [5.0, 650.0]) and El Soplao (top 95.4 %). Not checked because their benchmark coordinates are marked as written from memory: Dumont Dunes, Erfoud, Death Valley, NEEMO, HI-SEAS and Rio Tinto. A low percentage is a high place in the list; the check describes the ranking, it does not prove it.
## surface_classes_rover | show: ['mission_card', 'data_and_limits'] | status: None
    Ice sheets are not excluded: the funnel has no surface-type gate and the soft criteria have no surface-type term. 1,506 of the 10,337 ranked points are ice-sheet or snow-and-ice cells (snow and ice cover at least half of the cell, or the thermal class is 'hielo'); the best of them is number 1,089, 0 are in the top 50 and 0 among the five shown. Water is not excluded either: 1,145 ranked points are cells whose single commonest land-cover class is water (ESA CCI class 210, sea along a coast or lakes); the best is number 1, 12 are in the top 50 and 0 among the five shown (none).
## ties_at_top_rover | show: ['top5', 'data_and_limits'] | status: None
    254 points share the published final score of number 1 (100.00). Among tied points the order is by Earth cell id, so the five shown are not better than the other tied points.
## numbers_without_effect | show: ['data_and_limits'] | status: None
    Some numbers have no effect at this resolution. Gate limits that, removed on their own, would let no extra cell through: Rover: slope_300m_deg <= 15, rough_300m_m >= 5, rough_300m_m <= 650; Solar panels: mask_fraction >= 0.3, slope_deg <= 15, sun_elev_max_deg >= 10, sun_low_frac_0_5deg <= 0.25; Lunar base: mask_fraction >= 0.3. Values of the team's file mission_scoring.json that no code reads: Rover: the 'tolerances' block, the 'workable_days' block, the domain 'weights' (relieve, material, termico, luz, agua); Solar panels: the domain 'weights' (relieve, material, termico, luz, agua); Lunar base: the domain 'weights' (relieve, material, termico, luz, agua). The team's file is quoted as it is: these numbers are listed here, not removed.
## rover_no_new_variable | show: ['mission_card', 'data_and_limits'] | status: None
    Rover compares slope and roughness only. No further terrain variable is measured on both bodies, defined alike, meaningful for mobility and independent of these two: Diviner rock abundance, the obvious candidate, has a value in 0 of the 5,400 cells of the Rover lunar zone, and nothing equivalent exists for Earth.
## rover_tie | show: ['mission_card', 'top5', 'data_and_limits'] | status: None
    With slope and roughness alone, many sites differ from their lunar twin by less than the measurement error of the elevation models (ETOPO 2022 and LOLA; slope 0.27 degrees, roughness 17.4 m). Differences within that error are scored as equal, so 254 of the 10,337 paired sites share the top final score of 100.00; 241 remain after skipping cells that are half or more ice or less than half land. The tie is not a measured equality: the data cannot tell these sites apart. How the five were chosen: 241 sites (cells less than half ice and at least half land) tie at the top score: their difference in slope and roughness from their lunar twin is within the measurement error of the elevation models, so the data cannot tell them apart. The five shown are chosen among those tied sites (without cells that are half or more ice or less than half land) to be as far apart as possible: the first is the tied site with the highest similarity, then each next one is the tied site farthest from those already chosen. The full ranking itself is unchanged.
## rover_suitability_one_criterion | show: ['mission_card', 'data_and_limits'] | status: None
    In the Rover suitability, slope (45 %) and roughness (30 %) are nearly one criterion: over the 10,337 passing points their utilities have a Spearman correlation of 0.97. The team's weights are kept unchanged.
